// X64Runtime — the emitted program's PANIC RUNTIME, hand-assembled into `.text`.
//
// When the CPU raises `#DE` — on `a / 0`, or on the one other quotient that does not exist,
// `i64.min / -1` — Windows offers the fault to any vectored exception handler BEFORE it kills
// the process. This file emits that handler and the diagnostic it prints:
//
//     panic: integer divide by zero at rip=0x… diag_base=0x…
//     Stack trace:
//       in main
//       in mrt_start
//
// The two causes are ONE trap and two NTSTATUS codes, so which of the two lines above's first
// words appear is a classification the handler makes (`HardwareFaultKind`); `i64.min / -1` reads
// `panic: integer overflow` and is otherwise identical.
//
// The mechanism is the CPU's, not the compiler's: no divisor is ever tested. `idiv`
// traps for free, and a divide guarded by an explicit `cmp/jz` would cost every division
// in every program to diagnose the one that faults.
//
// WHY HAND-ASSEMBLED BYTES, NOT IR. Everything below is a `FunctionCodeChunk` built by
// calling X64Backend's encoders directly, rather than a Maxon or Std function the normal
// pipeline lowers. The Std tier at this milestone has only `arith` and `call` — it cannot
// load from an address, cannot store to one, and cannot name a machine register — so
// there is no Std spelling of "read CONTEXT.Rip" or "walk a saved-RBP chain". Growing the
// Std tier a memory/system band to express six runtime functions is a decision that
// belongs to R1 (P1.2), where the stdlib cone needs it for its own reasons; making it
// here, as a side effect of a fault handler, would pre-empt that with the narrowest
// possible evidence.
//
// These chunks are appended AFTER the module's own functions, so they are laid out after
// user code and before `__symtable` (which concat appends last). They are ordinary chunks
// in every other respect: `call rel32` between them resolves by name, `call [rip]` through
// the IAT resolves by slot, and `lea r64, [rip + <chunk>]` files a `CallFixup` like a call
// — a RIP-relative disp32 and a `call rel32` measure from the same place.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT:
//   - No CONTEXT REDIRECT. Rewriting the faulting thread's Rip/Rsp/Rbp to resume at a
//     separate diagnostic function and returning EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_EXECUTION is what a
//     STACK OVERFLOW needs, because there the faulting stack cannot be used to print
//     anything. Only `#DE` is converted, whose stack is intact, so the handler prints on
//     the spot and exits.
//   - No FAULT GLOBALS. Printing in place means Rip/Rbp/Rsp stay in this frame and travel
//     as ORDINARY ARGUMENTS — which is also why `mrt_fault_backtrace` takes three
//     parameters and reads no global. There is no writable-data-section pipeline here, so
//     this deletes a dependency rather than dodging one.
//   - No RE-ENTRANCY GUARD. A fault raised inside the diagnostic could only be an access
//     violation, which this handler declines (ContinueSearch), so it cannot recurse into
//     itself. The diagnostic divides nothing, so neither converted code can arise inside
//     it either.
//   - No GREEN-THREAD COUPLING. There is no scheduler until P1.5 and no `recover()`, so
//     there is no `gt` to find and nowhere to resume TO. The handler exits the process.
//
// CALLING CONVENTION. Every function here uses the Win64 ABI (args in RCX/RDX/R8/R9),
// including for its own internal calls — they interleave with kernel32 calls, and one
// convention throughout is one fewer thing to get wrong. This is NOT Maxon's custom ABI
// (primary return in R8); nothing here is callable from Maxon source, and `mrt_start`
// reaches the runtime through `mrt_runtime_init`, which takes no arguments.

// --- Win64 ABI ---
// The stack pointer must be 16-byte aligned AT a `call`. Every function below opens with
// `push rbp; mov rbp, rsp; sub rsp, <multiple of 16>`: entry is rsp ≡ 8 (mod 16), the push
// makes it ≡ 0, and a multiple-of-16 reservation keeps it there — so every `call` this
// file emits is aligned by construction. That matters more than it looks: kernel32 uses
// SSE internally, and a misaligned RSP surfaces as a crash deep inside WriteFile rather
// than anywhere near the mistake.
//
// The alignment itself is `X64PrologueEpilogue.StackFrameAlignment` — the SAME constant the
// compiled functions' frames are built from, not a local `= 16`. These frames interleave
// with those on one stack, so they are one fact, and a second copy could only ever drift
// into a silent misalignment.

// --- kernel32 ---
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED BECAUSE TWO MECHANISMS MATERIALIZE IT**: this file's hand-assembled chunks, which have no
// register allocator, and `StdToX64Conversion.win64StdHandleSelector`, which is register-allocated. They
// share nothing else, so the selector is exported rather than moved — one spelling, read from both.
//
// ⚠ Its POSIX counterpart lives in `StdLoweringShared.StderrFd` instead, beside its stdout twin: a
// DESCRIPTOR is a target-independent fact, where a Win32 HANDLE SELECTOR is an x64-Windows one and belongs
// with the rest of this table.
export let StdErrorHandle = 0xFFFFFFF4  // (DWORD)-12 — GetStdHandle's STD_ERROR_HANDLE

// AddVectoredExceptionHandler(First = 1, handler): 1 = call this handler BEFORE any
// previously registered one.
let VehHandlerFirst = 1

// A vectored handler's return values. `ContinueSearch` lets the OS's default disposition
// apply — which for a fault we did not convert is exactly what should happen.
let VehContinueSearch = 0

// A Windows NTSTATUS exception code. A DWORD — which is also the width `EXCEPTION_RECORD` stores it
// at and the width the thunk loads it with, so the comparand and the loaded value are one type.
typealias ExceptionCode = int(0 to u32.max)

// The two NTSTATUS codes Windows delivers for the `#DE` that `idiv` raises, and the only two this
// handler converts. `idiv` raises the SAME trap for a zero divisor and for a quotient that does not
// fit (`i64.min / -1`), and the CPU distinguishes them only by which code the OS then reports.
let ExcCodeIntDivideByZero = 0xC0000094  // EXCEPTION_INT_DIVIDE_BY_ZERO — a zero divisor.
let ExcCodeIntOverflow = 0xC0000095  // EXCEPTION_INT_OVERFLOW — an unrepresentable quotient.

// EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION — a read or write through an address the process does not own. Unlike the
// two above it is raised by no ONE instruction: any memory operand can take it, which is why the handler
// converts it on the CODE alone and reads nothing else out of the record.
let ExcCodeAccessViolation = 0xC0000005

// EXCEPTION_POINTERS field offsets (the struct RCX points at).
let ExcPtrsOffExceptionRecord = 0x00
let ExcPtrsOffContextRecord = 0x08

// EXCEPTION_RECORD.ExceptionCode — a DWORD at offset 0, so it must be read with a 32-bit
// load. A 64-bit load would drag in the neighbouring ExceptionFlags field; a SIGN-extended
// read would turn 0xC0000094 into a negative number that matches nothing.
let ExcRecOffExceptionCode = 0x00

// CONTEXT field offsets (x64). The three registers a backtrace needs.
let ContextOffRsp = 0x098
let ContextOffRbp = 0x0A0
let ContextOffRip = 0x0F8

// --- Hex formatting: the x64 writer's own two constants ---
//
// The FORMAT is `Targets/Shared/BacktraceFormat`'s (16 digits, the ASCII bases, the buffer). These two
// are how THIS instruction stream reaches a nibble and belong to no other writer.

// log2 of the 4 bits a hex digit carries: `index * 4` is emitted as `shl index, 2`. Spelled as its own
// constant because a shift COUNT is not a bit-width — deriving one from the other (`bits / 2`) is right
// for 4-bit digits and silently wrong for any other width.
let HexBitsPerDigitShift = 2

// x64 shifts the chosen nibble down by a VARIABLE count, so the digit has to be masked out of what sits
// below it.
let HexNibbleMask = 0xF

// ==========================================================================
// The x64-WINDOWS console write path — its constants, its `.data` cache, and its names
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐ **x64-WINDOWS ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION.** A console is a Win32 object: Linux writes bytes at a
// descriptor with one `write` syscall and arm64/wasm reach their own primitives, so none of this has a
// counterpart on those lanes and none of them emits `mrt_write_stream`.

// `CP_UTF8` — the code page a Maxon `String`'s bytes are in, named at each conversion rather than set once
// on the console. The console's own output code page belongs to the console: it is shared with the parent
// shell and with every sibling attached to it, it cannot be restored without writing state a concurrent
// process may be depending on, and it says nothing at all when the stream is a pipe or a file.
let ConsoleCodePageUtf8 = 65001

// ⛔ **`dwFlags = 0`, NEVER `MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS`.** A Maxon `String` is not validated UTF-8
// (`stdlib/String.maxon`) and `__Builtins.writeStdout(anyByteArray.managed)` is legal, so an ill-formed
// byte must become U+FFFD rather than fail the call and take a diagnostic with it.
let ConsoleWidenNoFlags = 0

// The UTF-16 units one conversion produces, which is also the bytes one slice consumes — ONE constant for
// both, because that is what makes the buffer big enough.
//
// ⭐ **units ≤ input bytes, ALWAYS**: a 1-, 2- or 3-byte sequence widens to one unit and a 4-byte sequence
// to two, so K input bytes can never produce more than K units. `ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER` is therefore
// unreachable by construction rather than by a check.
//
// ⚠ **32 IS A STACK BUDGET, NOT A THROUGHPUT CHOICE.** `mrt_write_stream` is un-guarded and free-rides on
// the green-thread guard margin together with the rest of the un-guarded `mrt_` diagnostic chain, so the
// `2 × K` bytes the buffer costs come out of what that chain leaves. Raising it is legal exactly as far as
// `requireWriteStreamFitsGreenThreadStack` says — which is the point of that certificate: the number below
// is a reading, and a reading with no gate under it is a comment that rots.
let ConsoleWideBufferUnits = 32

// The width of one UTF-16 code unit. It appears once as a `lea` index scale and once in the buffer's size;
// there is no 16-bit load or store anywhere here, because only the kernel ever reads the units.
let Utf16UnitBytes = 2

// A UTF-8 CONTINUATION byte is `10xxxxxx`. A slice cut at `ConsoleWideBufferUnits` may land inside a
// sequence, so the cut walks LEFT off any continuation bytes before converting — at most three steps,
// which is the longest tail a well-formed sequence has.
let Utf8ContinuationMask = 0xC0
let Utf8ContinuationTag = 0x80
let Utf8MaxContinuationBackoff = 3

// The OUTGOING stack-argument slots `mrt_write_stream` fills, named rather than left as bare 0/1 for the
// reason `StdToX64Conversion`'s `CreateProcess*Slot` family is: an index at an `[rsp+disp]` store says
// nothing about which argument of which call is being written. `win64OutgoingArgDisp` turns each into
// `[rsp + shadow + slot*8]`, which is exactly where the GT syscall shim copies them from.
let WriteFileOverlappedArgSlot = 0  // WriteFile arg 5: lpOverlapped = NULL
let WriteConsoleReservedArgSlot = 0  // WriteConsoleW arg 5: lpReserved = NULL
let WidenBufferArgSlot = 0  // MultiByteToWideChar arg 5: lpWideCharStr
let WidenBufferUnitsArgSlot = 1  // MultiByteToWideChar arg 6: cchWideChar

// ⭐⭐ **ONE `.data` CELL PER STANDARD STREAM, HOLDING WHAT THE PROBE ANSWERED.** Console-ness is a property
// of the handle for the life of the process, so it is asked once per stream and read on every write after
// that. `unprobed` is 0 because `.data` starts zeroed, so "not yet asked" costs no initializer.
//
// ⭐ **THE RACE IS BENIGN AND THAT IS WHY THERE IS NO LOCK.** Two threads writing one cell write the SAME
// answer — `GetConsoleMode` on one handle cannot say two things — so a lost update costs at most one extra
// probe. A byte is written whole by the ISA, so no reader can see half a verdict.
enum ConsoleVerdict
	unprobed = 0
	isConsole = 1
	notConsole = 2
end 'ConsoleVerdict'

// ⭐ **THE THREE CELLS ARE ONE RUN, INDEXED BY `StdInputHandleSelector - selector`** — the property
// `ConsoleRuntime` already states for the POSIX descriptors, so the map is one subtraction. They are three
// 1-byte slots pushed CONSECUTIVELY into one `DataSectionEntryArray`, and
// `GlobalDataTable.sortBySizeDescendingStable` appends each size class in declaration order, so they are
// adjacent in `.data` by construction rather than by luck.
//
// ⚠ **STANDARD INPUT GETS A CELL IT NEVER USES**, and one byte buys a total map: with a two-cell run based
// at the OUTPUT selector, an input selector would index one byte BEFORE the run, and nothing structural
// would say so. Its cell is therefore the run's BASE, because index 0 is standard input's.
let ConsoleProbeStdinLabel = b"__mrt_console_probe_stdin"
let ConsoleProbeStdoutLabel = b"__mrt_console_probe_stdout"
let ConsoleProbeStderrLabel = b"__mrt_console_probe_stderr"

// ⭐ **THE CACHE IS LAID OUT FOR EVERY x64-WINDOWS PROGRAM, ON THE TARGET ALONE.** Its reader is
// `mrt_write_stream`, which every such binary carries: a program that never prints still panics, and a
// panic writes to stderr. There is no usage bit that could gate it more tightly.
//
// ⚠ **1-BYTE SLOTS, AND THE WIDTH IS LOAD-BEARING.** `layOut` orders `.data` by storage size descending,
// so a 1-byte runtime slot lands after every user global of every width — which is what keeps
// `RequiredData`'s prefix-from-byte-0 rule pinning the same bytes it pinned before. An 8-byte cell here
// would displace every sub-word user global in the corpus.
export function x64WindowsRuntimeGlobals(target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if target.cpu != CpuArch.x64 or target.os != Os.windows 'notThisLane'
		return out
	end 'notThisLane'

	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: ConsoleProbeStdinLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins, valueType: StdType.i8)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: ConsoleProbeStdoutLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins, valueType: StdType.i8)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: ConsoleProbeStderrLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins, valueType: StdType.i8)
	return out
end 'x64WindowsRuntimeGlobals'

// --- Runtime function names ---
//
// The names every lane shares are `Targets/Shared/BacktraceFormat`'s. These three are x64-Windows's own:
// a VEH callback, the trampoline the x86-64 kernel demands, and the one stream writer.

// ⭐ **THE ONE WRITE PATH BOTH PRODUCERS REACH.** The isel lowers `osWriteStdout`/`osWriteStderr` to a call
// of this name and the panic writer tail-calls it, so the console probe, the widening and the two loops
// exist exactly once. EXPORTED because `StdToX64Conversion` names it.
export let WriteStreamName = b"mrt_write_stream"

// The vectored exception handler `AddVectoredExceptionHandler` registers. A different name from the
// POSIX `FaultHandlerName` because the two are reached differently — one is a callback the OS invokes
// with `EXCEPTION_POINTERS`, the other a signal handler.
let FaultThunkName = b"mrt_fault_thunk"

// The rt_sigreturn trampoline `sa_restorer` points at. x86-64 has no kernel signal trampoline, so the
// kernel demands a restorer address to build the return frame — mandatory even though this handler never
// returns through it. Just `mov eax, 15; syscall` (rt_sigreturn).
let LinuxSigreturnName = b"mrt_rt_sigreturn"

// ==========================================================================
// The chunk assembler
// ==========================================================================

typealias RuntimeStringLabelMap = Map with (ByteArray, BlockId)
typealias RuntimeStringLabelArray = Array with BlockId
typealias RuntimeStringBytesArray = Array with ByteArray

// Assembles ONE hand-written runtime function into a FunctionCodeChunk: the buffers an
// emitter needs, a label allocator for its branches and strings, and the frame contract.
//
// It carries no per-instruction methods on purpose. X64Backend's encoders are the
// instruction vocabulary and they are called directly (`encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code,
// opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, …)`); a method per instruction would be a wrapper whose whole
// body is one call with a constant swapped, which is the same objection X64Backend already
// makes to a `encodeMov`/`encodeSub`/`encodeCmp` family. What lives here is only what
// needs STATE: labels, the string pool, and the frame.
export type X64RuntimeAsm
	export var code as ByteArray
	export var callFixups as CallFixupArray
	export var iatRelocs as RelocationArray

	// `.data` references: the Linux host surface reaches its errno word through `lea reg, [rip + disp32]`,
	// whose displacement only the executable writer can fill in.
	export var rdataRelocs as RdataRelocationArray
	export var rel32Fixups as ChunkRel32FixupArray
	export var labelOffsets as BlockOffsetTable
	export var nextLabel as BlockId

	// Strings are laid down at the END of the chunk, past the `ret`, so they are data the
	// function can address but never execute. Deduped by content within the chunk.
	export var stringLabelOf as RuntimeStringLabelMap
	export var stringLabels as RuntimeStringLabelArray
	export var stringBytes as RuntimeStringBytesArray
	export var frameBytes as StackSize
	export var slotAreaBytes as StackSize

	// What `beginFunction` reserved for OUTGOING stack arguments, kept so `outgoingArgDisp` can refuse a
	// slot outside it.
	export var outgoingArgBytes as StackSize

	// Open a function: allocate its frame and emit `push rbp; mov rbp, rsp; sub rsp, N`.
	//
	// `slots` is how many 8-byte locals the body needs; `outgoingStackArgBytes` is space for
	// call arguments BEYOND the four the Win64 ABI passes in registers. The frame is laid
	// out from the bottom up — shadow space, outgoing args, then the slots — and rounded to
	// 16, which is what keeps every `call` in the body aligned (see FrameAlignment).
	export static function beginFunction(slots StackSize, outgoingStackArgBytes StackSize = 0) returns X64RuntimeAsm
		let slotAreaBytes = slots * SpillSlotBytes
		let frameBytes = alignUpStack(ShadowSpaceBytes + outgoingStackArgBytes + slotAreaBytes, alignment: StackFrameAlignment)

		// The prologue is emitted into the buffer BEFORE the struct owns it — `Self{…}` is
		// then a single immutable construction rather than a build-then-mutate.
		var code = ByteArray.create()
		encodePushReg(code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
		encodeRmRegDirect(code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rbp, src: X64Register.rsp, wide: true)

		// No certificate: every runtime chunk's frame is a hand-counted handful of slots — the widest is
		// `mrt_write_stream`'s UTF-16 buffer at 0xC0 bytes, an order of magnitude short of a page — so none
		// can reach the guard page. A chunk that ever wanted a page-sized buffer would panic here rather
		// than emit a frame that steps over it, and would then have to name a free scratch register, which
		// is a claim about ITS entry contract that this shared builder cannot make on its behalf.
		//
		// ⚠ THE GUARD PAGE IS NOT THE ONLY CEILING. A chunk emitted for x64-windows is also un-guarded
		// against a GREEN THREAD's 2 KB stack, which is a far tighter bound; `buildWriteStreamChunk`
		// certifies itself against it, because the budget is a property of the CHAIN a chunk sits in and
		// not of any one frame.
		encodePrologue(code, stackSize: frameBytes, probeScratchIsFree: false)

		return Self{
			code: code,
			callFixups: CallFixupArray.create(),
			iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(),
			rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(),
			rel32Fixups: ChunkRel32FixupArray.create(),
			labelOffsets: BlockOffsetTable.create(),
			nextLabel: 0,
			stringLabelOf: RuntimeStringLabelMap.create(),
			stringLabels: RuntimeStringLabelArray.create(),
			stringBytes: RuntimeStringBytesArray.create(),
			frameBytes: frameBytes,
			slotAreaBytes: slotAreaBytes,
			outgoingArgBytes: outgoingStackArgBytes
		}
	end 'beginFunction'

	// Close a function: the epilogue (the exact mirror of the prologue — `add rsp` undoes
	// the `sub`, `pop rbp` the `push`, so rsp comes home by balanced arithmetic), the `ret`,
	// then the string pool, then resolve every chunk-local rel32 now that all of it is
	// placed. Neither the epilogue nor `ret` touches RAX, so a function's return value
	// survives them.
	//
	// ONE exit per function, by construction: an early-out branches to a label placed just
	// before this epilogue rather than emitting a second one.
	export function finishFunction(name ByteArray) returns FunctionCodeChunk
		encodeEpilogue(self.code, stackSize: self.frameBytes)
		encodePopReg(self.code, reg: X64Register.rbp)
		self.code.push(OpcodeRet)

		for (iter, bytes) in self.stringBytes.withIterator() 'strings'
			let label = try self.stringLabels.get(iter.index()) otherwise panic("finishFunction: stringLabels and stringBytes are appended together in stringLabel(), so index {iter.index()} must exist in both")
			self.placeLabel(label)
			self.code.append(bytes)
			self.code.push(0)  // the NUL every consumer here scans for
		end 'strings'

		resolveChunkRel32Fixups(self.code, labelOffsets: self.labelOffsets, rel32Fixups: self.rel32Fixups)

		return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(name, code: self.code, callFixups: self.callFixups, rdataRelocs: self.rdataRelocs, iatRelocs: self.iatRelocs, rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
	end 'finishFunction'

	// Mint a fresh chunk-local label id.
	export function newLabel() returns BlockId
		let id = self.nextLabel
		self.nextLabel = id + 1
		return id
	end 'newLabel'

	// Fix `label` at the current offset. Every branch to it — already emitted or not — is
	// patched by `finishFunction`.
	export function placeLabel(label BlockId)
		self.labelOffsets.place(label, value: self.code.count())
	end 'placeLabel'

	// The label of a NUL-terminated string constant in this chunk, laying it out on first
	// use. Deduped by content: two `lea`s of the same bytes share one copy.
	export function stringLabel(bytes ByteArray) returns BlockId
		let existing = try self.stringLabelOf.get(bytes) otherwise 'fresh'
			let label = self.newLabel()
			self.stringLabelOf.upsert(bytes, value: label)
			self.stringLabels.push(label)
			self.stringBytes.push(bytes)
			return label
		end 'fresh'

		return existing
	end 'stringLabel'

	// The `[rbp + disp]` displacement of local slot `index`. Slots grow DOWNWARD from rbp,
	// so they occupy the top of the frame and can never collide with the shadow space or an
	// outgoing argument at the bottom — which a callee is free to clobber.
	export function slotDisp(index StackSize) returns Imm32
		let offset = (index + 1) * SpillSlotBytes

		if offset > self.slotAreaBytes 'outOfFrame'
			panic("X64RuntimeAsm.slotDisp: slot {index} needs {offset} bytes but the frame reserved only {self.slotAreaBytes} — `beginFunction` was asked for too few slots")
		end 'outOfFrame'

		return -offset as Imm32
	end 'slotDisp'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `[rsp + disp]` DISPLACEMENT OF OUTGOING STACK-ARGUMENT SLOT `index`, BOUNDS-CHECKED — the
	// COMPLETE site, so a reservation too small for the calls a chunk makes cannot be silent.** The
	// reservation is a number a builder works out from the widest import it names, and nothing structural
	// ties that arithmetic to the stores; a chunk that grew a call with one more stack word than it reserved
	// would write into `spillSlotBaseDisp`'s first spill slot and corrupt a live value with no fault. Every
	// outgoing-argument store in this file goes through here, so the disagreement is a BUILD-TIME panic
	// naming the slot and the reservation instead.
	//
	// The rule itself is `win64OutgoingArgDisp`'s and is not restated: this adds the certificate, not a
	// second addressing convention.
	export function outgoingArgDisp(index MachineWord) returns Imm32
		let regionEnd = (index + 1) * SpillSlotBytes

		if (regionEnd as StackSize) > self.outgoingArgBytes 'outOfRegion'
			panic("X64RuntimeAsm.outgoingArgDisp: outgoing stack-argument slot {index} ends at byte {regionEnd} of the outgoing region but `beginFunction` reserved only {self.outgoingArgBytes} — the store would land on spill slot 0 and corrupt a live value. Raise the chunk's `outgoingStackArgBytes` to cover the widest call it makes")
		end 'outOfRegion'

		return win64OutgoingArgDisp(index)
	end 'outgoingArgDisp'
end 'X64RuntimeAsm'

// ==========================================================================
// Shared emit fragments
// ==========================================================================

// `cmp reg, imm` — the zero/constant test every guard below is written in terms of. x64's
// group-1 immediate is SIGN-extended to 64 bits, which is what makes `cmp rax, -1` (the
// "no symbol found" marker) compare against the full-width value and not 0x00000000FFFFFFFF.
export function emitCmpRegImm(asm X64RuntimeAsm, reg X64Register, imm Imm32)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtCmp, reg: reg, imm: imm)
end 'emitCmpRegImm'

// `lea dest, [rip + <string>]` — load the address of a chunk-local string constant.
// Exported: the Linux host surface takes the address of its own chunk-local constants the same way — the
// `/proc/self/exe` path it hands `readlinkat`, and every panic message a green-thread chunk names.
export function emitLeaString(asm X64RuntimeAsm, dest X64Register, bytes ByteArray)
	encodeLeaRegLabel(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, dest: dest, targetLabel: asm.stringLabel(bytes), site: PatchSite.x64RuntimeLabelDisp32)
end 'emitLeaString'

// Print a chunk-local string constant: `lea rcx, [rip + s]; call mrt_write_cstr_stderr`.
// The single most repeated three lines in this file.
function emitPrintString(asm X64RuntimeAsm, bytes ByteArray)
	emitLeaString(asm, dest: X64Register.rcx, bytes: bytes)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteStderrName)
end 'emitPrintString'

// `dest = <the runtime address of `mrt_start`>` — the backtrace's `text_base`. Every
// symbolizable address is expressed relative to it, and concat asserts it is the FIRST
// chunk in `.text` so that offset can never be negative.
function emitLeaTextBase(asm X64RuntimeAsm, dest X64Register)
	encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, dest: dest, targetName: EntryPointName)
end 'emitLeaTextBase'

// `rax = strlen(cstrReg)`, leaving `cstrReg` holding the string start. A C string does not carry its
// length and the emitted program links no CRT, so this walk is the only way to it.
//
// Shared by the WINDOWS writer (which hands the length to `WriteFile`) and the LINUX one (which hands
// it to `write(2)`), because how long a string is is not an OS question. It had been open-coded in
// both, instruction for instruction: nothing made the two agree, and an off-by-one corrected in one
// of them is a truncated or over-long diagnostic from the other, not a compile error.
//
// Clobbers rdx (the byte under test).
//
// Exported: the Linux host surface measures an environment variable's value the same way, for the same
// reason — a `char*` out of the `envp` vector carries no length either.
export function emitCstrLenIntoRax(asm X64RuntimeAsm, cstrReg X64Register)
	let lenLoop = asm.newLabel()
	let lenDone = asm.newLabel()

	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: cstrReg, wide: true)

	asm.placeLabel(lenLoop)
	encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rax, disp: 0)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rdx, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: lenDone)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: 1)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: lenLoop)

	asm.placeLabel(lenDone)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: cstrReg, wide: true)
end 'emitCstrLenIntoRax'

// `dest = <the runtime address of `__symtable`>`. Doubles as the `.text` upper bound:
// concat lays the table down immediately after the last function, so `__symtable -
// mrt_start` IS the size of the code.
function emitLeaSymtable(asm X64RuntimeAsm, dest X64Register)
	encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, dest: dest, targetName: SymbolTableName)
end 'emitLeaSymtable'

// Everything a fault diagnostic prints AFTER its opening words: the two ADDRESSES that recover the
// ASLR slide (`rip=` is already open when this is called, `diag_base=` is opened here), and the
// symbolized backtrace under them.
//
// Shared by the Windows thunk and the Linux handler because only the CLASSIFICATION and the exit
// route differ between the two OSes — this middle is identical, tags and call sequence and all, and two
// copies of a format is two chances for one of them to move.
function emitFaultAddressesAndBacktrace(asm X64RuntimeAsm, ripSlot StackSize, rbpSlot StackSize, rspSlot StackSize)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(ripSlot), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteHexName)
	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: FaultDiagBaseTag)
	emitLeaTextBase(asm, dest: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteHexName)
	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: FaultLineTerminator)

	// mrt_fault_backtrace(rcx = rip, rdx = rbp, r8 = rsp) — prints `Stack trace:`, frame 0 from the
	// faulting rip, then the saved-rbp chain bounded to the faulted stack.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(ripSlot), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(rbpSlot), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r8, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(rspSlot), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: FaultBacktraceName)
end 'emitFaultAddressesAndBacktrace'

// Terminate the process with `PanicExitCode` — the LAST instruction of every terminal diagnostic in
// this file, and the ONE thing about a panic that is still an OS question once the message and the
// backtrace are printed. `route` is `x64OsCallKind`'s own answer, derived once in
// `appendX64RuntimeChunks` and threaded down, so this file cannot grow a third opinion about which OSes
// the x64 backend serves. The two fault chunks name their own route as a literal instead, and that is
// not a second opinion: `buildFaultThunkChunk` is a VEH callback and `buildLinuxFaultHandlerChunk`
// answers a `rt_sigaction` signal — each is one OS by its own content, not by a decision made elsewhere.
//
// Shared by all THREE terminal paths — the Windows fault thunk, the Linux fault handler, and the
// OS-parametric `mrt_panic` — for `emitFaultAddressesAndBacktrace`'s reason: the first two had each
// open-coded their two instructions, and `mrt_panic` becoming OS-parametric would have made that two
// copies apiece. A panic's exit code and the register it travels in are one fact per OS.
//
// ⚠ THE ARGUMENT REGISTER IS PART OF THE DOOR, NOT A DETAIL BESIDE IT. Win64 passes `ExitProcess`'s
// code in ECX; the Linux syscall ABI passes `exit_group`'s in EDI. That is why the immediate load sits
// inside each arm rather than above the match — exactly the split `lowerOsExit` makes, and for the
// same reason.
function emitPanicProcessExit(asm X64RuntimeAsm, route X64OsCallKind)
	match route 'route'
		importCall then emitWin64PanicProcessExit(asm)
		syscall then emitLinuxPanicProcessExit(asm)
	end 'route'
end 'emitPanicProcessExit'

// Windows: `ExitProcess(PanicExitCode)` through the IAT. Never returns, so nothing follows it.
function emitWin64PanicProcessExit(asm X64RuntimeAsm)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: PanicExitCode)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ExitProcess))
end 'emitWin64PanicProcessExit'

// Linux: `exit_group(PanicExitCode)` via a raw syscall. `exit_group` and not `exit` for the reason
// `X64LinuxRuntime.X64LinuxSyscallExitGroup` states — a panic ends the thread GROUP.
function emitLinuxPanicProcessExit(asm X64RuntimeAsm)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdi, value: PanicExitCode)
	encodeX64Syscall(asm.code, number: X64LinuxSyscallExitGroup)
end 'emitLinuxPanicProcessExit'

// Emit the two bounds checks that decide whether `addrReg` (already reduced to a `.text`
// offset) names code this image can symbolize, jumping to `rejectLabel` if not:
// `0 <= offset < __symtable - mrt_start`. Signed comparisons throughout — a return address
// into a system DLL lands either below the image (negative) or far above the code, and both
// ends must reject. `scratchA`/`scratchB` are clobbered.
//
// Shared by the walker (which applies it to every return address) and the fault backtrace
// (which applies it to the faulting RIP), because "is this offset inside our code" is one
// question and a second copy of it could answer differently.
function emitTextBoundsGuard(asm X64RuntimeAsm, offsetReg X64Register, scratchA X64Register, scratchB X64Register, textBaseSlot StackSize, symtableSlot StackSize, rejectLabel BlockId)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: offsetReg, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.less, targetBlockId: rejectLabel)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: scratchA, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(symtableSlot), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: scratchB, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(textBaseSlot), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: scratchA, src: scratchB, wide: true)  // scratchA = textSize
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: offsetReg, src: scratchA, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.greaterEqual, targetBlockId: rejectLabel)
end 'emitTextBoundsGuard'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_write_stream(rcx = GetStdHandle selector, rdx = bytes, r8 = length) -> rax = bytes accepted
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE WRITE PATH OF AN x64-WINDOWS PROGRAM.** Every byte `print`, `printError` and the panic
// runtime put on a standard stream leaves through here, so the console probe and the UTF-16 widening are
// written once. A second copy in the isel would be free to drift from this one on exactly the buffer
// arithmetic that is hardest to get right.
//
// It answers the number of bytes of the CALLER's buffer that were accepted, which is what
// `__Builtins.writeStdout` returns.
//
// ⛔ **THE CONSOLE ROAD RETURNS THE INPUT BYTE COUNT, NOT `WriteConsoleW`'s OUT-PARAM**, which counts
// UTF-16 CODE UNITS — a different quantity that would make `print` report a different number on a console
// than on a pipe for the same string. `specs/print-error-function.md` builds exit codes out of the answer.
//
// Slots: 0 selector, 1 handle, 2 bytes, 3 length, 4 offset, 5 slice, 6 units, 7 units already accepted,
// 8 the Win32 out-param word, 9.. the UTF-16 buffer.
let WriteStreamSlotSelector = 0
let WriteStreamSlotHandle = 1
let WriteStreamSlotBytes = 2
let WriteStreamSlotLength = 3
let WriteStreamSlotOffset = 4
let WriteStreamSlotSlice = 5
let WriteStreamSlotUnits = 6
let WriteStreamSlotDone = 7
let WriteStreamSlotOutWord = 8
let WriteStreamSlotWide = 9

// The outgoing stack-argument words the frame reserves: the WIDEST of the three Win32 calls made from it,
// because ONE outgoing region serves all of them. Read from the same constants the GT syscall shim copies
// by (`StdToX64Conversion`), so the reservation and the copy cannot disagree. A FUNCTION because a global
// initializer may not call one.
//
// ⚠ **THIS LIST IS NOT WHAT MAKES THE RESERVATION RIGHT — `X64RuntimeAsm.outgoingArgDisp` IS.** Naming the
// calls here is an enumeration, and an enumeration a fourth call can be added to without touching. The
// STORES are the complete site, and each of them refuses a slot outside what was reserved, so a list that
// fell behind the chunk's call sites is a build-time panic rather than a frame written past its region.
function writeStreamOutgoingArgWords() returns MachineWord
	var widest = WriteFileStackArgWords

	if WriteConsoleStackArgWords > widest 'consoleWrite'
		widest = WriteConsoleStackArgWords
	end 'consoleWrite'

	if MultiByteToWideCharStackArgWords > widest 'widen'
		widest = MultiByteToWideCharStackArgWords
	end 'widen'

	return widest
end 'writeStreamOutgoingArgWords'

// The 8-byte slots the UTF-16 buffer occupies.
function writeStreamWideSlots() returns StackSize
	return (alignUp(ConsoleWideBufferUnits * Utf16UnitBytes, alignment: SpillSlotBytes) / SpillSlotBytes) as StackSize
end 'writeStreamWideSlots'

// The two words a `call` and the prologue's `push rbp` put below a chunk's own frame — what a footprint
// costs beyond the reservation `beginFunction` made.
let ChunkEntryWordsBytes = 2 * StackWordBytes

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE REST OF THE UN-GUARDED CHAIN ALREADY SPENDS.** The deepest chain of un-guarded frames on
// this lane is the fault diagnostic — `mrt_fault_thunk` → `mrt_fault_backtrace` → `mrt_walk_frames` →
// `mrt_print_frame` → `mrt_write_hex64` → `mrt_write_cstr_stderr` → `mrt_write_stream` — and each frame
// costs `beginFunction`'s reservation plus `ChunkEntryWordsBytes`: 96 + 96 + 112 + 96 + 96 + 48 = 544.
// The green-thread syscall shim adds at most 56 more on the GT stack across a call (the pushed r10 and
// `emitGtExitSyscall`'s slow frame), which no chunk's own frame accounts for.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS MEASURED FROM THOSE BUILDERS AND NOTHING RE-DERIVES IT**, so widening any un-guarded chunk's
// frame leaves this number stale in the OPTIMISTIC direction. Re-measure it at the builder that grew.
let UnguardedDiagnosticChainBytes = 544 + 56

// ⛔⛔ **THE CEILING ON `ConsoleWideBufferUnits`, AND IT IS THE GREEN-THREAD STACK RATHER THAN THE GUARD
// PAGE.** This chunk is emitted with no stack-guard check, so it spends the margin the guard leaves below
// a green thread's 2 KB stack (`GtRuntime.gtStackGuardMargin`) — and it spends it AFTER the diagnostic
// chain above has. A buffer raised for throughput would otherwise overflow that stack silently, on the one
// path where the symptom is a corrupted neighbouring green thread rather than a fault.
//
// The frame is read back from `beginFunction` rather than recomputed, so this certificate cannot come to
// disagree with the frame it certifies.
function requireWriteStreamFitsGreenThreadStack(frameBytes StackSize, target Target)
	let footprint = (frameBytes as ParsedInt) + ChunkEntryWordsBytes
	let budget = gtStackGuardMargin(target) - UnguardedDiagnosticChainBytes

	if footprint > budget 'overflowsGreenThreadStack'
		panic("requireWriteStreamFitsGreenThreadStack: mrt_write_stream's frame is {footprint} bytes but the un-guarded chain leaves only {budget} of the {gtStackGuardMargin(target)}-byte green-thread guard margin — a green thread printing through it would run off its 2 KB stack with no fault. Lower ConsoleWideBufferUnits ({ConsoleWideBufferUnits} units cost {ConsoleWideBufferUnits * Utf16UnitBytes} bytes of frame), or raise GtRuntime.GtStackGuardMarginBytes and measure the self-compile against the >5% rule")
	end 'overflowsGreenThreadStack'
end 'requireWriteStreamFitsGreenThreadStack'

// `dest = &<this stream's probe cell>` — the whole selector-to-cell map, which is one subtraction because
// the three `GetStdHandle` selectors descend as the three cells ascend (see `ConsoleProbeStdinLabel`).
// Clobbers rax and r11, so `dest` may be neither.
function emitConsoleCellPtr(asm X64RuntimeAsm, dest X64Register)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotSelector), wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r11, value: StdInputHandleSelector)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.r11, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegGlobal(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: dest, label: ConsoleProbeStdinLabel)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(asm.code, dest: dest, base: dest, index: X64Register.r11, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
end 'emitConsoleCellPtr'

// ⛔⛔ **A WIN32 `BOOL` OR `int` IS A DWORD, SO THE HIGH HALF OF RAX IS UNDEFINED WHEN THE CALL RETURNS**,
// and every comparison in this file is `cmp r64, imm32` (`encodeGrp1RegImm32` emits REX.W). `mov eax, eax`
// zero-extends, which makes the value the register holds the one the kernel actually answered.
//
// ⛔ It is not cosmetic at `MultiByteToWideChar`: a garbage high half would survive the zero test, be stored
// as `units`, and hand `WriteConsoleW` a count that reads far past the 64-byte frame buffer. The other two
// sites test a BOOL, where garbage above a zero low half would merely take the fallback — the same answer
// for the wrong reason, which is why all three narrow rather than only the one that must.
function emitNarrowRaxToDword(asm X64RuntimeAsm)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: false)
end 'emitNarrowRaxToDword'

// `WriteFile(handle, bytes + offset, length - offset, &out, NULL)` — the raw-byte write, leaving the
// accepted count in the out-param slot. Shared by the NOT-A-CONSOLE road, which is the whole of what it
// does, and by the console road's fallback, which reaches it with a non-zero offset.
//
// ⚠ The out-param word is zeroed BEFORE the call and read back WHOLE. `lpNumberOfBytesWritten` is a
// `DWORD*` and the slot is eight bytes, so without the zeroing the high half would carry whatever the
// frame last held; with it the count arrives zero-extended and exact, and a `WriteFile` that FAILS having
// written nothing reads back 0 rather than garbage.
function emitWriteStreamRawWrite(asm X64RuntimeAsm, usesGt bool)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: asm.outgoingArgDisp(WriteFileOverlappedArgSlot), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotBytes), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r8, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotLength), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord))

	emitGtGatedIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, callFixups: asm.callFixups, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.WriteFile), usesGt: usesGt)
end 'emitWriteStreamRawWrite'

// ⛔⛔ **EVERY IMPORT CALL HERE GOES THROUGH `emitGtGatedIatCall`, NOT `encodeIatCall`.** A hand-assembled
// chunk is not routed through the green-thread shim by `emitFunctionChunk` the way a compiled function's
// `iatCall` is, so calling the IAT directly would run a Win32 call on a 2 KB green-thread stack and would
// delete the `blocking` syscall bracket from the hottest slot there is — every `print` from a green thread.
function buildWriteStreamChunk(usesGt bool, target Target) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	if ConsoleWideBufferUnits <= Utf8MaxContinuationBackoff 'bufferTooSmallToBackOff'
		panic("buildWriteStreamChunk: ConsoleWideBufferUnits is {ConsoleWideBufferUnits}, which is not more than the {Utf8MaxContinuationBackoff}-byte continuation back-off — a cut slice could then be walked down to zero bytes and the outer loop would never advance")
	end 'bufferTooSmallToBackOff'

	let wideSlots = writeStreamWideSlots()
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(((WriteStreamSlotWide + wideSlots) as StackSize), outgoingStackArgBytes: ((writeStreamOutgoingArgWords() * SpillSlotBytes) as StackSize))
	requireWriteStreamFitsGreenThreadStack(asm.frameBytes, target: target)

	// The buffer grows UPWARD in memory from the LAST of the slots it occupies, because slots grow downward
	// from rbp — `mrt_write_hex64`'s idiom.
	let wideDisp = asm.slotDisp(((WriteStreamSlotWide + wideSlots - 1) as StackSize))

	let done = asm.newLabel()
	let cellReady = asm.newLabel()
	let verdictReady = asm.newLabel()
	let notAConsole = asm.newLabel()
	let outerLoop = asm.newLabel()
	let sliceChosen = asm.newLabel()
	let backOffLoop = asm.newLabel()
	let innerLoop = asm.newLabel()
	let sliceWritten = asm.newLabel()
	let rawFallback = asm.newLabel()
	let consoleDone = asm.newLabel()

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotSelector), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotBytes), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotLength), srcReg: X64Register.r8)

	// Nothing to write is answered before any OS call — which is also what keeps `cbMultiByte = 0`, the one
	// value `MultiByteToWideChar` reports as FAILURE, out of reach. rax is the answer on every exit, so the
	// zero it is tested against here is already the one this exit returns.
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.r8, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: done)

	// The handle is asked for on every write, unlike the verdict below: `SetStdHandle` may replace a standard
	// handle at any point, so a cached one could name a stream the program has since redirected, and
	// `GetStdHandle` is a read of a word the process already holds.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotSelector), wide: true)
	emitGtGatedIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, callFixups: asm.callFixups, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetStdHandle), usesGt: usesGt)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotHandle), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// Is this stream a console? Asked once per stream and cached; every later write reads the cell.
	emitConsoleCellPtr(asm, dest: X64Register.r10)
	encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: ConsoleVerdict.unprobed.rawValue)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: cellReady)

	// ⛔ **`GetConsoleMode`, NOT `GetFileType`, AND `> NUL` IS WHAT SEPARATES THEM.** A redirect to `NUL`
	// answers `FILE_TYPE_CHAR` — the same word a real console answers — while `WriteConsoleW` FAILS on that
	// handle, so a file-type test would send that program's output down a road that discards it and reports
	// success. `GetConsoleMode` succeeds for a console and for nothing else, which is the question here.
	// `specs/terminal-color-detection.md`'s colour predicate asks a genuinely different question — "is a
	// terminal on the other end" — and is right to use `GetFileType`; the two must not be folded together.
	//
	// The mode word lands in the Win32 out-param slot and is never read: the CALL's verdict is the answer.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord))
	emitGtGatedIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, callFixups: asm.callFixups, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.GetConsoleMode), usesGt: usesGt)
	emitNarrowRaxToDword(asm)

	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r8, value: ConsoleVerdict.notConsole.rawValue)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: verdictReady)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r8, value: ConsoleVerdict.isConsole.rawValue)

	asm.placeLabel(verdictReady)
	emitConsoleCellPtr(asm, dest: X64Register.r10)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg8(asm.code, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0, srcReg: X64Register.r8)

	// Both roads arrive with r10 = this stream's cell, so the cached and the freshly probed paths take the
	// same instructions from here on.
	asm.placeLabel(cellReady)
	encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.r10, disp: 0)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: ConsoleVerdict.isConsole.rawValue)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: notAConsole)

	// --- the CONSOLE road: widen a slice at a time and hand the units over ---
	asm.placeLabel(outerLoop)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotLength), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.lessEqual, targetBlockId: consoleDone)

	// slice = min(remaining, K). An uncut slice is whole by definition and needs no back-off.
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: ConsoleWideBufferUnits)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.belowEqual, targetBlockId: sliceChosen)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: ConsoleWideBufferUnits)

	// ⭐ **A CUT SLICE WALKS LEFT WHILE THE BYTE IT CUTS AT IS A CONTINUATION BYTE**, so a multi-byte
	// sequence is never split across two conversions and never widened into a run of U+FFFDs.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE BYTE UNDER TEST IS THE ONE THE NEXT SLICE WOULD START WITH — `bytes[offset + slice]`, THE
	// FIRST EXCLUDED BYTE — NOT THE LAST INCLUDED ONE.** A slice that ends ON a continuation byte may hold a
	// whole sequence, and one that ends on a LEAD byte has certainly split one. MEASURED against a real
	// console with the other reading: `é` cut between its two bytes reached the screen as two replacement
	// characters, because its lead byte read as "not a continuation" and the walk stopped there.
	//
	// ⚠ Reading at the cut is IN BOUNDS precisely because the slice was cut: this arm runs only when
	// `remaining > ConsoleWideBufferUnits`, so `offset + slice` is still inside the caller's buffer.
	//
	// ⭐ **TERMINATION.** The walk is bounded at `Utf8MaxContinuationBackoff` steps — the longest tail a
	// well-formed sequence has — and the builder refuses a buffer that small, so `slice >=
	// ConsoleWideBufferUnits - 3 >= 1` on this road and `slice = remaining` on the other. The outer loop
	// therefore advances `offset` by at least one byte every iteration.
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rcx, wide: true)

	asm.placeLabel(backOffLoop)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rcx, imm: Utf8MaxContinuationBackoff)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.aboveEqual, targetBlockId: sliceChosen)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotBytes), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.r11, src: X64Register.r9, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.r11, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeMovzxRegByteBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.r11, disp: 0)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtAnd, reg: X64Register.r9, imm: Utf8ContinuationMask)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.r9, imm: Utf8ContinuationTag)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: sliceChosen)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtSub, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 1)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtAdd, reg: X64Register.rcx, imm: 1)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: backOffLoop)

	asm.placeLabel(sliceChosen)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotSlice), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// MultiByteToWideChar(CP_UTF8, 0, bytes + offset, slice, wide, K).
	// ⛔ `cbMultiByte` is the EXPLICIT slice length and never -1: an embedded NUL is a byte committed specs
	// push through this door, and -1 would stop at the first one.
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: wideDisp)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: asm.outgoingArgDisp(WidenBufferArgSlot), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: ConsoleWideBufferUnits)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: asm.outgoingArgDisp(WidenBufferUnitsArgSlot), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: ConsoleCodePageUtf8)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, value: ConsoleWidenNoFlags)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r8, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotBytes), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotSlice), wide: true)
	emitGtGatedIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, callFixups: asm.callFixups, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.MultiByteToWideChar), usesGt: usesGt)
	emitNarrowRaxToDword(asm)

	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: rawFallback)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotUnits), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotDone), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// WriteConsoleW accepts a PREFIX of the units it is offered, so the slice is offered again from where it
	// stopped until every unit has landed.
	asm.placeLabel(innerLoop)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotUnits), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotDone), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.lessEqual, targetBlockId: sliceWritten)

	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotHandle), wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: wideDisp)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rdx, index: X64Register.r11, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byTwo)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rsp, disp: asm.outgoingArgDisp(WriteConsoleReservedArgSlot), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord))
	emitGtGatedIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, callFixups: asm.callFixups, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.WriteConsoleW), usesGt: usesGt)
	emitNarrowRaxToDword(asm)

	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: rawFallback)

	// ⛔ **TRUE WITH ZERO UNITS ACCEPTED MUST LEAVE THIS LOOP**, or it spins for ever offering the same units
	// to a console that is taking none.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: rawFallback)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotDone), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.r11, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotDone), srcReg: X64Register.r11)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: innerLoop)

	asm.placeLabel(sliceWritten)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotSlice), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: outerLoop)

	// ⛔⛔ **A REFUSED CONVERSION OR A FAILED CONSOLE WRITE NEVER DROPS BYTES** — it writes the RAW remaining
	// bytes at the handle instead. Mojibake is recoverable; a vanished diagnostic is not. A console that
	// accepted part of a slice before failing sees at most `ConsoleWideBufferUnits` bytes twice, and
	// duplication is the safe direction.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE ANSWER IS WHAT ACTUALLY LANDED — the bytes the console took plus the bytes the fallback
	// moved — and NOT the caller's length.** Every slice before this point was accepted whole (the inner
	// loop leaves only when every unit has landed), so `offset` is exactly that prefix; `WriteFile`'s
	// out-param is the rest. Answering the full length here would report a write nothing performed if the
	// fallback failed too, which is the one road on which this function can honestly say "less".
	asm.placeLabel(rawFallback)
	emitWriteStreamRawWrite(asm, usesGt: usesGt)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOffset), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r11, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeAddRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.r11, wide: true)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: done)

	asm.placeLabel(consoleDone)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotLength), wide: true)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: done)

	// --- the NOT-A-CONSOLE road: one `WriteFile`, answering the count the kernel reports ---
	asm.placeLabel(notAConsole)
	emitWriteStreamRawWrite(asm, usesGt: usesGt)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(WriteStreamSlotOutWord), wide: true)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
	return asm.finishFunction(WriteStreamName)
end 'buildWriteStreamChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_write_cstr_stderr(rcx = NUL-terminated string)
// ==========================================================================

// Write a C string to stderr. The length is measured here because a C string does not carry one; everything
// else — the handle, the console probe, the widening, the empty-string case — belongs to `mrt_write_stream`
// and is not repeated here. rcx survives the length walk, so the string needs no frame slot.
function buildWriteStderrChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	emitCstrLenIntoRax(asm, cstrReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.rcx, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: StdErrorHandle)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteStreamName)

	return asm.finishFunction(WriteStderrName)
end 'buildWriteStderrChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_write_hex64(rcx = value)
// ==========================================================================

// Print `value` as 16 zero-padded lowercase hex digits. Formats into a frame buffer and
// hands it to `mrt_write_cstr_stderr` — one WriteFile, not sixteen.
//
// Slots: 0 = value, 1 = digit index, 2..4 = the 17-byte output buffer.
function buildWriteHexChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotValue = 0
	let SlotIndex = 1
	let SlotBuffer = 2
	let bufferSlots = alignUp(HexBufferBytes, alignment: SpillSlotBytes) / SpillSlotBytes
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(((SlotBuffer + bufferSlots) as StackSize))

	let loop = asm.newLabel()
	let loopDone = asm.newLabel()
	let isDigit = asm.newLabel()
	let store = asm.newLabel()

	// The buffer occupies slots SlotBuffer.. upward in memory, so its first byte is at the
	// LOWEST of them — slots grow downward from rbp, so that is the LAST slot index.
	let bufferDisp = asm.slotDisp(((SlotBuffer + bufferSlots - 1) as StackSize))

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotValue), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	asm.placeLabel(loop)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: HexDigitsPerWord)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.greaterEqual, targetBlockId: loopDone)

	// nibble = (value shr (60 - 4*index)) and 0xF — most significant digit first.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotValue), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeGrp2RegImm8(asm.code, ext: Grp2ExtShl, reg: X64Register.r8, count: HexBitsPerDigitShift)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: HexTopDigitShift)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.r8, wide: true)

	// SHR, not SAR: these are the raw bits of an address, not a signed quantity — an
	// arithmetic shift would smear the top bit across every digit of a high address.
	encodeGrp2RegCl(asm.code, ext: Grp2ExtShr, reg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeGrp1RegImm32(asm.code, ext: Grp1ExtAnd, reg: X64Register.rdx, imm: HexNibbleMask)

	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rdx, imm: HexDigitTen)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.less, targetBlockId: isDigit)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rdx, disp: HexAsciiLowerAMinusTen)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: store)
	asm.placeLabel(isDigit)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rdx, disp: HexAsciiZero)

	asm.placeLabel(store)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: bufferDisp)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.r9, index: X64Register.rax, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg8(asm.code, base: X64Register.r9, disp: 0, srcReg: X64Register.rdx)

	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: 1)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: loop)

	asm.placeLabel(loopDone)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: bufferDisp)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg8(asm.code, base: X64Register.r9, disp: HexDigitsPerWord, srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.r9, wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteStderrName)

	return asm.finishFunction(WriteHexName)
end 'buildWriteHexChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_panic_print_frame(rcx = text_offset, rdx = symtable_ptr)
// ==========================================================================

// Print one frame as `  in <name>\n`, resolving `text_offset` through the symbol table.
// A linear scan keeping the LAST entry whose codeOffset is <= text_offset — which is the
// GREATEST such entry because the table ascends (asserted where it is built).
//
// Slots: 0 = text_offset, 1 = symtable_ptr, 2 = count, 3 = index, 4 = best name offset.
function buildPrintFrameChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotTextOffset = 0
	let SlotSymtable = 1
	let SlotCount = 2
	let SlotIndex = 3
	let SlotBestName = 4
	assertSymtabStride()
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(5)

	let lookup = asm.newLabel()
	let next = asm.newLabel()
	let lookupDone = asm.newLabel()
	let unknown = asm.newLabel()
	let newline = asm.newLabel()

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotTextOffset), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)

	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: FrameLinePrefix)

	// count = *(u32*)symtable — a 32-bit load, which zero-extends. Loading 8 bytes and
	// masking would be the same value in two instructions.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: 0, wide: false)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotCount), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// -1 = "no symbol matched yet". A real nameOffset is a u32 read from the table, so it
	// can never collide with this.
	encodeMovRegImm64(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: u64.max)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotBestName), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	asm.placeLabel(lookup)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotCount), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.greaterEqual, targetBlockId: lookupDone)

	// entry = symtable + 4 + index*8
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeGrp2RegImm8(asm.code, ext: Grp2ExtShl, reg: X64Register.r8, count: SymtabEntryShift)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.r9, index: X64Register.r8, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r9, base: X64Register.r9, disp: SymtabOffEntries)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.r9, disp: SymtabEntryOffCodeOffset, wide: false)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotTextOffset), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.less, targetBlockId: next)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.r9, disp: SymtabEntryOffNameOffset, wide: false)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotBestName), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	asm.placeLabel(next)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: 1)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotIndex), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: lookup)

	asm.placeLabel(lookupDone)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotBestName), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: -1)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: unknown)

	// name_ptr = symtable + best (nameOffset is relative to the table's start)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), wide: true)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rcx, index: X64Register.rax, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteStderrName)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: newline)

	asm.placeLabel(unknown)
	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: UnknownFrameName)

	asm.placeLabel(newline)
	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: FrameLineTerminator)

	return asm.finishFunction(PrintFrameName)
end 'buildPrintFrameChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_panic_walk_frames(rcx = start_frame, rdx = stack_low, r8 = stack_high)
// ==========================================================================

// Walk the saved-RBP chain from `start_frame`, printing up to 32 symbolized frames.
//
// ONE walker, two callers, distinguished by `stack_high`, and the reason it is one function and
// not two: `stack_high == 0` trusts the chain (a SOFTWARE panic, whose frames it built itself),
// while a non-zero `stack_high` additionally requires every frame pointer to lie in
// [stack_low, stack_high) and to STRICTLY ASCEND — so a hardware fault that corrupted RBP degrades
// to a short trace rather than a second fault inside the diagnostic.
//
// ⭐ BOTH ARMS ARE LIVE, on BOTH x64 lanes. The software arm prints EVERY range-check backtrace the
// compiler emits, through `mrt_panic`, and it does NO bounds validation — measured safe because every
// frame it walks is a compiled prologue this backend built (including a green thread's, whose chain
// terminates on the zeroed saved-rbp `__gt_trampoline` inherits from a fresh GT stack).
//
// Slots: 0 = frame, 1 = stack_low, 2 = stack_high, 3 = counter, 4 = text_base,
//        5 = symtable, 6 = next frame.
function buildWalkFramesChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotFrame = 0
	let SlotStackLow = 1
	let SlotStackHigh = 2
	let SlotCounter = 3
	let SlotTextBase = 4
	let SlotSymtable = 5
	let SlotNextFrame = 6
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(7)

	let loop = asm.newLabel()
	let getReturnAddr = asm.newLabel()
	let advance = asm.newLabel()
	let done = asm.newLabel()

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFrame), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotStackLow), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotStackHigh), srcReg: X64Register.r8)

	emitLeaTextBase(asm, dest: X64Register.rax)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotTextBase), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	emitLeaSymtable(asm, dest: X64Register.rax)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: MaxBacktraceFrames)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotCounter), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	asm.placeLabel(loop)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotCounter), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: done)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: -1)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotCounter), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// A null frame pointer ends the chain — `mrt_start`'s saved rbp is whatever the loader
	// left, which on the initial thread is zero.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFrame), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: done)

	// stack_high == 0 disables range validation (the software-panic caller).
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotStackHigh), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rdx, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: getReturnAddr)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotStackLow), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.less, targetBlockId: done)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotStackHigh), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.greaterEqual, targetBlockId: done)

	asm.placeLabel(getReturnAddr)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFrame), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: FrameOffReturnAddr, wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: done)

	// text_offset = (return_addr - 1) - text_base
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: -ReturnAddrSymbolBias)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotTextBase), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	emitTextBoundsGuard(asm, offsetReg: X64Register.rax, scratchA: X64Register.rdx, scratchB: X64Register.r8, textBaseSlot: SlotTextBase, symtableSlot: SlotSymtable, rejectLabel: done)

	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: PrintFrameName)

	// next = [frame]
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFrame), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: FrameOffSavedFramePointer, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotNextFrame), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotStackHigh), wide: true)
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rdx, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: advance)

	// Require next > frame. The stack grows down, so a caller's frame is always at a HIGHER
	// address; a link that does not ascend is a corrupt chain, and following it would be the
	// wild read this walk exists to avoid.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFrame), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.greaterEqual, targetBlockId: done)

	asm.placeLabel(advance)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotNextFrame), wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFrame), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: loop)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
	return asm.finishFunction(WalkFramesName)
end 'buildWalkFramesChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_fault_backtrace(rcx = fault_rip, rdx = fault_rbp, r8 = fault_rsp)
// ==========================================================================

// Print `Stack trace:` and a symbolized backtrace for a hardware fault. There is no `gt` lookup —
// the context arrives as three arguments.
//
// Frame 0 is the faulting instruction, symbolized from `fault_rip` with NO bias: a fault
// RIP already points AT the instruction that trapped, whereas a return address points past
// its call and needs the -1 (see ReturnAddrSymbolBias). Frames 1..N come from walking the
// saved-RBP chain, bounded to [fault_rsp, fault_rsp + 64 MiB).
//
// Slots: 0 = fault_rip, 1 = fault_rbp, 2 = fault_rsp, 3 = text_base, 4 = symtable.
function buildFaultBacktraceChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotFaultRip = 0
	let SlotFaultRbp = 1
	let SlotFaultRsp = 2
	let SlotTextBase = 3
	let SlotSymtable = 4
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(5)

	let walk = asm.newLabel()

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRip), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRbp), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRsp), srcReg: X64Register.r8)

	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: BacktraceHeader)

	emitLeaTextBase(asm, dest: X64Register.rax)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotTextBase), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	emitLeaSymtable(asm, dest: X64Register.rax)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// Frame 0. A fault whose RIP is in a system DLL has no local symbol, so the bounds guard
	// skips it and the trace starts at the RBP chain instead.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRip), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotTextBase), wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeSubRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	emitTextBoundsGuard(asm, offsetReg: X64Register.rax, scratchA: X64Register.rdx, scratchB: X64Register.r8, textBaseSlot: SlotTextBase, symtableSlot: SlotSymtable, rejectLabel: walk)

	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotSymtable), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: PrintFrameName)

	asm.placeLabel(walk)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRbp), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRsp), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.r8, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRsp), wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: StackWindowBytes)
	encodeLeaRegBaseIndexScale(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r8, base: X64Register.r8, index: X64Register.rax, scale: MemoryIndexScale.byOne)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WalkFramesName)

	return asm.finishFunction(FaultBacktraceName)
end 'buildFaultBacktraceChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_fault_thunk(rcx = EXCEPTION_POINTERS*)  — the vectored exception handler
// ==========================================================================

// ONE classification arm: "if the OS's code equals `excCode`, this fault is `kind`". A match records
// that kind's panic prefix in `prefixSlot` and jumps to `classified`; a miss falls through to the
// next arm. Both arms go through this function so the code→words mapping is decided in exactly one
// place, and so the sign-extension trap is dodged by one technique rather than by two.
//
// ⚠ THE COMPARAND TRAVELS THROUGH A REGISTER. x64's group-1 immediate is SIGN-extended, so
// `cmp rax, 0xC0000094` would compare against 0xFFFFFFFFC0000094 and never match the zero-extended
// DWORD the caller just loaded. Both codes have bit 31 set, so this is not a quirk of one of them.
function emitFaultCodeArm(asm X64RuntimeAsm, codeReg X64Register, scratch X64Register, excCode ExceptionCode, kind HardwareFaultKind, prefixSlot StackSize, classified BlockId)
	let next = asm.newLabel()

	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: scratch, value: excCode as MachineWord)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeCmpRmReg, dest: codeReg, src: scratch, wide: true)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.notEqual, targetBlockId: next)

	emitLeaString(asm, dest: scratch, bytes: faultPanicLinePrefix(kind))
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(prefixSlot), srcReg: scratch)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: classified)

	asm.placeLabel(next)
end 'emitFaultCodeArm'

// The callback `AddVectoredExceptionHandler` invokes on a hardware fault, BEFORE the OS
// applies its default disposition. Win64 ABI: the EXCEPTION_POINTERS in RCX, a LONG back
// in EAX.
//
// It converts the two integer `#DE` codes and the ACCESS VIOLATION, and NOTHING ELSE. Every
// other code returns EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH so the OS still handles it: a fault handler
// that swallowed faults it was not asked about would turn every unrelated crash into this
// one's diagnostic. (STACK OVERFLOW is not converted: it would be the same one `cmp` per code, but
// it also needs a guard page the handler can run on, which none of the three here do.)
//
// ⚠ **THE ACCESS-VIOLATION ARM IS WHAT MAKES THIS HANDLER TESTABLE AT ALL.** Every other route
// to it is a compiler BUG, so until `__Builtins.forceSegfault()` existed the whole thunk was
// code no spec case could reach on purpose. `FaultProbeRuntime` is that door.
//
// Slots: 0 = EXCEPTION_POINTERS*, 1 = fault_rip, 2 = fault_rbp, 3 = fault_rsp, 4 = panic prefix.
function buildFaultThunkChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotExcPtrs = 0
	let SlotFaultRip = 1
	let SlotFaultRbp = 2
	let SlotFaultRsp = 3
	let SlotPanicPrefix = 4
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(5)

	let continueSearch = asm.newLabel()
	let classified = asm.newLabel()

	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotExcPtrs), srcReg: X64Register.rcx)

	// code = *(u32*)(p->ExceptionRecord), then one arm per code we convert. An arm that matches
	// leaves its panic prefix in `SlotPanicPrefix` and jumps to `classified`; falling out of the last
	// arm means this is not our fault, and the OS keeps it.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rcx, disp: ExcPtrsOffExceptionRecord, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rax, disp: ExcRecOffExceptionCode, wide: false)
	emitFaultCodeArm(asm, codeReg: X64Register.rax, scratch: X64Register.rdx, excCode: ExcCodeIntDivideByZero, kind: HardwareFaultKind.divideByZero, prefixSlot: SlotPanicPrefix, classified: classified)
	emitFaultCodeArm(asm, codeReg: X64Register.rax, scratch: X64Register.rdx, excCode: ExcCodeIntOverflow, kind: HardwareFaultKind.overflow, prefixSlot: SlotPanicPrefix, classified: classified)
	emitFaultCodeArm(asm, codeReg: X64Register.rax, scratch: X64Register.rdx, excCode: ExcCodeAccessViolation, kind: HardwareFaultKind.accessViolation, prefixSlot: SlotPanicPrefix, classified: classified)
	encodeJmp(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, targetBlockId: continueSearch)

	asm.placeLabel(classified)

	// Stash the faulting Rip/Rbp/Rsp. The faulted frames are still mapped and untouched —
	// the handler runs on the same thread, BELOW the faulting RSP — so this chain is walkable
	// exactly as it stands.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotExcPtrs), wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rcx, disp: ExcPtrsOffContextRecord, wide: true)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rax, disp: ContextOffRip, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRip), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rax, disp: ContextOffRbp, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRbp), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rdx, base: X64Register.rax, disp: ContextOffRsp, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFaultRsp), srcReg: X64Register.rdx)

	// The panic line opens with whichever prefix the classification chose; the rest of the diagnostic
	// is the OS-neutral one. The harness strips the addresses, an operator reads them.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rcx, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotPanicPrefix), wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteStderrName)
	emitFaultAddressesAndBacktrace(asm, ripSlot: SlotFaultRip, rbpSlot: SlotFaultRbp, rspSlot: SlotFaultRsp)

	// ExitProcess(1) — never returns. There is no `recover()` and no scheduler to resume
	// onto, so a converted fault is terminal.
	emitPanicProcessExit(asm, route: X64OsCallKind.importCall)

	// Falls through: unreachable after ExitProcess, and the single `ret` every other code
	// reaches directly.
	asm.placeLabel(continueSearch)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: VehContinueSearch)

	return asm.finishFunction(FaultThunkName)
end 'buildFaultThunkChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_runtime_init()
// ==========================================================================

// Register the fault handler. `mrt_start` calls this before `main`, so the very first
// instruction of user code is already covered.
//
// It exists as its own function because `mrt_start` is built from TargetOps, and the Target
// tier has no op for "the address of a function" — the argument `AddVectoredExceptionHandler`
// needs. Rather than mint a TargetOp variant for one hand-built call site (a new variant has
// to be band-appended, and every `match` over the union pays for it forever), the address is
// taken HERE, in a chunk that assembles its own bytes, and `mrt_start` reaches it with the
// `callDirect` it already has. This is also where the runtime's later prelude lands.
function buildRuntimeInitChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: VehHandlerFirst)
	encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, dest: X64Register.rdx, targetName: FaultThunkName)
	encodeIatCall(asm.code, iatRelocs: asm.iatRelocs, slotIndex: iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.AddVectoredExceptionHandler))

	return asm.finishFunction(RuntimeInitName)
end 'buildRuntimeInitChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_panic(rcx = NUL-terminated cstr)  — the callable software-panic entry
// ==========================================================================

// The panic a P1.9 range guard calls when a value falls outside its typealias. Prints the caller's
// message, a `Stack trace:` header, a symbolized backtrace, and exits 1. It ASSEMBLES the existing
// fault-path primitives — it does not reimplement any of them:
//   (1) `mrt_write_cstr_stderr` prints the cstr in RCX (the pass bakes the whole `panic at …\n` line);
//   (2) the same writer prints the `Stack trace:` header;
//   (3) `mrt_panic_walk_frames`'s SOFTWARE arm (`stack_high == 0`, which trusts the saved-rbp chain the
//       compiled frames build rather than range-validating it) walks from THIS frame — so `[rbp+8]`,
//       the return address into the function whose guard fired, is the first symbolized frame and
//       `mrt_panic` never appears in the trace;
//   (4) under `--coverage` only, `__cov_dump(ABORTED)` publishes the counters this run did reach;
//   (5) `exit(PanicExitCode)` through `exitRoute`'s OS door.
//
// This is the reader the walker's software arm was written for and had been waiting on (X64Runtime's
// header noted the arm was unreached until P1.2's `panic()`; the range guard reaches it first).
//
// ⭐ THE ONLY OS-PARAMETRIC CHUNK IN THIS FILE, and steps (1)–(3) are why: every primitive it calls is
// either OS-NEUTRAL (the walker, the frame printer, `__symtable` — one chunk each, appended to BOTH
// lanes) or reached BY NAME through a writer each lane appends its own copy of under the same
// `mrt_write_cstr_stderr` label. So the assembly above is identical on Windows and Linux, and only
// step (4) — the process exit — has an OS in it. `exitRoute` carries exactly that and nothing more.
// Every other chunk here is either shared outright or named for its one OS (`buildLinux…`).
//
// ⚠ RBP MUST SURVIVE STEPS (1) AND (2), because step (3) loads it as the walk's seed — and a walk over
// a clobbered frame pointer prints plausible nonsense, which on a fault path is strictly worse than
// printing nothing. It survives on BOTH lanes, and NOT because of a callee-saved convention this file
// could only assume: `X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction` gives every chunk a `push rbp` prologue and
// `finishFunction` the matching `pop rbp`, so each writer restores it by balanced arithmetic. This is
// what makes the Linux writer safe here despite ending in a `syscall`, which CLOBBERS RCX AND R11 —
// neither of which this chunk reads across a call (the message in RCX is consumed by step (1) itself,
// and step (3) re-loads RCX from RBP). It takes NO slots for the same reason: nothing is live across
// the calls.
function buildPanicChunk(exitRoute X64OsCallKind, dumpCoverage bool) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	// (1) The caller's message — already in RCX, NUL-terminated and newline-suffixed by the pass.
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WriteStderrName)

	// (2) The `Stack trace:` header.
	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: BacktraceHeader)

	// (3) Walk from this frame with the software arm: rcx = current rbp, rdx = stack_low (unused when
	// stack_high == 0), r8 = stack_high = 0.
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rcx, src: X64Register.rbp, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.r8, src: X64Register.r8, wide: true)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: WalkFramesName)

	// (4) The counters, on an instrumented build only.
	emitCoverageAbortDump(asm, dumpCoverage: dumpCoverage)

	// (5) exit(PanicExitCode) — the one step with an OS in it.
	emitPanicProcessExit(asm, route: exitRoute)

	return asm.finishFunction(PanicName)
end 'buildPanicChunk'

// ⭐⭐ **PUBLISH THE COUNTERS AN ABORTING RUN DID REACH, MARKED `ABORTED`.** The entry stub's dump is the
// only other one, and it is on a path a panic never takes: without this, a program that dies loses every
// count it accumulated — which is the run a reader most wants coverage for — and no reader ever sees a
// PARTIAL report at all. `__cov_dump` allocates nothing and takes no lock, which is what makes it
// callable from a path the program is already failing on (`CoverageRuntime`'s header).
//
// ⛔ **LAST, AFTER THE MESSAGE AND THE BACKTRACE.** A panic's own diagnostics are worth more than its
// counters, so nothing the dump does can come between the reader and them.
//
// ⛔ **ONLY THE DELIBERATE ABORT DUMPS — THE CPU-FAULT PATH STAYS UNHOOKED.** A fault handler runs on an
// already-compromised stack, where opening a file turns a diagnosable fault into a silent kill. Such a
// run leaves no data file, which every driver already reports as a run that wrote none.
//
// ⚠ **THE STACK IS ALIGNED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY CORRECTION.** `beginFunction` lays `push rbp` over a
// frame rounded to `StackFrameAlignment`, so RSP is 16-byte aligned at every call in this chunk — the two
// above included. A re-alignment here would be the defect: it would MIS-align the call it was meant to fix.
function emitCoverageAbortDump(asm X64RuntimeAsm, dumpCoverage bool)
	if not dumpCoverage 'notInstrumented'
		return
	end 'notInstrumented'

	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rcx, value: MxcovStatusAborted as MachineWord)
	encodeCallDirect(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: CoverageDumpName)
end 'emitCoverageAbortDump'

// ==========================================================================
// Assembly
// ==========================================================================

// ==========================================================================
// The Linux (x64) fault runtime
// ==========================================================================
//
// THE LINUX TWIN OF THE WINDOWS FAULT RUNTIME, and it reuses most of it. `mrt_fault_backtrace`,
// `mrt_panic_walk_frames`, `mrt_panic_print_frame` and `mrt_write_hex64` are OS-NEUTRAL — they call each
// other and `mrt_write_cstr_stderr` BY NAME and touch no IAT — so the Linux runtime appends them
// unchanged. Only THREE chunks differ from Windows, all because Linux reaches the OS through raw syscalls
// rather than kernel32 imports: the stderr writer (`write` vs a call into `mrt_write_stream`), the fault ENTRY (a
// SIGFPE handler reading the ucontext vs a VEH callback reading EXCEPTION_POINTERS), and `runtime_init`
// (rt_sigaction vs AddVectoredExceptionHandler). The first and last carry the SAME names as their Windows
// twins (`mrt_write_cstr_stderr`, `mrt_runtime_init`), so the reused chunks and `mrt_start` resolve to
// whichever the OS build appended — never both in one binary.

// mrt_rt_sigreturn — the `sa_restorer` trampoline. Raw `mov eax, 15; syscall`, with NO prologue: the
// kernel enters a restorer with the signal frame already on the stack, so a push/frame would corrupt it.
// Built directly (not through X64RuntimeAsm, which opens a frame). Never actually executed while this
// handler exits, but the kernel demands the address exists to build the return frame at all.
function buildLinuxSigreturnTrampolineChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var code = ByteArray.create()
	encodeX64Syscall(code, number: X64LinuxSyscallRtSigreturn)
	return FunctionCodeChunk.createWith(LinuxSigreturnName, code: code, callFixups: CallFixupArray.create(), rdataRelocs: RdataRelocationArray.create(), iatRelocs: RelocationArray.create(), rdataConstants: GlobalDataTable.create())
end 'buildLinuxSigreturnTrampolineChunk'

// mrt_write_cstr_stderr (LINUX) — rcx = NUL-terminated string. The Linux twin of `buildWriteStderrChunk`:
// the same strlen, then a single `write(2, cstr, len)` syscall where the Windows one hands the bytes to
// `mrt_write_stream`. Keeps the file's internal Win64 convention (the string arrives in rcx).
//
// ⭐ It measures, writes and guards the empty case in ONE body because a descriptor takes bytes as they
// are: there is nothing on this lane for a stream writer to decide, so there is no `mrt_write_stream`
// beneath it to decide it.
function buildLinuxWriteStderrChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	let done = asm.newLabel()

	// rcx (the string start) is held untouched across the walk; len = rax - rcx.
	emitCstrLenIntoRax(asm, cstrReg: X64Register.rcx)

	// An empty string is a no-op — skip `write(2, buf, 0)`.
	emitCmpRegImm(asm, reg: X64Register.rax, imm: 0)
	encodeJcc(asm.code, rel32Fixups: asm.rel32Fixups, cond: X64CondCode.equal, targetBlockId: done)

	// write(2, cstr, len): args go in rdi/rsi/rdx. Move len (rax) and cstr (rcx) into place BEFORE the
	// syscall number overwrites eax.
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeMovRmReg, dest: X64Register.rsi, src: X64Register.rcx, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdi, value: StderrFd)
	encodeX64Syscall(asm.code, number: X64LinuxSyscallWrite)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
	return asm.finishFunction(WriteStderrName)
end 'buildLinuxWriteStderrChunk'

// mrt_fault_handler (LINUX) — the SIGFPE handler. Kernel SA_SIGINFO entry ABI: rdi=signum, rsi=siginfo*,
// rdx=ucontext*. The Linux twin of `buildFaultThunkChunk`: reads the faulting RIP/RBP/RSP from the
// ucontext (not a Windows CONTEXT), prints the SAME panic line + backtrace, and terminates with
// exit_group(1) (not ExitProcess). It NEVER returns, which is why the sigaction needs no restorer.
//
// ⚠ IT CANNOT MAKE THE WINDOWS THUNK'S DISTINCTION, AND THE LIMITATION IS THE KERNEL'S. The Windows
// thunk tells a zero divisor from an unrepresentable quotient (`i64.min / -1`) because the two arrive
// as different NTSTATUS codes. Linux offers only `siginfo->si_code`, and x86's `exc_divide_error`
// reports `FPE_INTDIV` for EVERY `#DE` — the CPU never told the kernel which of the two causes fired,
// so there is nothing to branch on. MEASURED, not inferred: this handler was instrumented to print
// the raw `si_code` and both a zero divisor and `i64.min / -1` read 1 (`FPE_INTDIV`). Recovering the
// distinction would mean decoding the faulting `idiv`'s ModRM at the ucontext RIP to find which
// register held the divisor — a disassembler in hand-assembled bytes, welded to one encoding, whose
// failure mode is a CONFIDENTLY WRONG diagnostic rather than a missing one. So this handler prints
// the divide-by-zero wording for every SIGFPE it receives, which is also why a stray floating-point
// trap would read that way; `specs/safety.md` states both halves and gates the overflow case to
// `x64-windows`.
//
// Slots: 0=rip, 1=rbp, 2=rsp.
function buildLinuxFaultHandlerChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotRip = 0
	let SlotRbp = 1
	let SlotRsp = 2
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(3)

	// Stash the faulting RIP/RBP/RSP from the ucontext (rdx) BEFORE any call clobbers rdx.
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rdx, disp: X64LinuxUContextRipOffset, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotRip), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rdx, disp: X64LinuxUContextRbpOffset, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotRbp), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeLoadRegBaseDisp(asm.code, destReg: X64Register.rax, base: X64Register.rdx, disp: X64LinuxUContextRspOffset, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotRsp), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// The panic line — through the SAME prefix builder the Windows thunk picks from, so the two OSes
	// cannot drift on the wording and the harness's strip-from-` at rip=0x` leaves an identical line.
	emitPrintString(asm, bytes: faultPanicLinePrefix(HardwareFaultKind.divideByZero))
	emitFaultAddressesAndBacktrace(asm, ripSlot: SlotRip, rbpSlot: SlotRbp, rspSlot: SlotRsp)

	// exit_group(1) — never returns. A converted fault is terminal (no recover(), no scheduler).
	emitPanicProcessExit(asm, route: X64OsCallKind.syscall)

	return asm.finishFunction(FaultHandlerName)
end 'buildLinuxFaultHandlerChunk'

// mrt_runtime_init (LINUX) — registers the SIGFPE handler via rt_sigaction. `mrt_start` calls this before
// `main`, so the first user instruction is already covered. The Linux twin of `buildRuntimeInitChunk`.
// Builds a `struct sigaction` on the stack: the handler address, SA_SIGINFO (so the handler receives the
// ucontext), a NULL restorer (the handler exits, so rt_sigreturn is never reached), and an empty mask —
// then rt_sigaction(SIGFPE, &act, NULL, 8). Slots 0..3 are the 32-byte struct, ascending from the LOWEST
// slot so `&act` (= the lowest field, sa_handler) is `slotDisp(3)`.
function buildLinuxRuntimeInitChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotHandler = 3  // rbp-32 = &act = sa_handler (offset 0)
	let SlotFlags = 2  // rbp-24 = sa_flags (offset 8)
	let SlotRestorer = 1  // rbp-16 = sa_restorer (offset 16)
	let SlotMask = 0  // rbp-8 = sa_mask (offset 24)
	var asm = X64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(4)

	encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, dest: X64Register.rax, targetName: FaultHandlerName)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandler), srcReg: X64Register.rax)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rax, value: X64LinuxSigactionFlags)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotFlags), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// sa_restorer = &mrt_rt_sigreturn — MANDATORY on x86-64: the kernel builds the signal return frame from
	// it, and a missing/NULL restorer is a KERNEL SIGSEGV during delivery (measured), before the handler.
	encodeLeaRegFuncAddr(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, dest: X64Register.rax, targetName: LinuxSigreturnName)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotRestorer), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// sa_mask = 0 (block nothing additional during the handler).
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rax, src: X64Register.rax, wide: true)
	encodeStoreBaseDispReg(asm.code, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotMask), srcReg: X64Register.rax)

	// rt_sigaction(SIGFPE, &act, NULL, 8): rdi=SIGFPE, rsi=&act (the lowest field's address), rdx=0
	// (oldact), r10=8 (the kernel's sigset_t size).
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rdi, value: LinuxSignalFpe)
	encodeLeaRegRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.rsi, base: X64Register.rbp, disp: asm.slotDisp(SlotHandler))
	encodeRmRegDirect(asm.code, opcode: OpcodeXorRmReg, dest: X64Register.rdx, src: X64Register.rdx, wide: true)
	encodeMovRegImm32(asm.code, dest: X64Register.r10, value: LinuxSigsetBytes)
	encodeX64Syscall(asm.code, number: X64LinuxSyscallRtSigaction)

	return asm.finishFunction(RuntimeInitName)
end 'buildLinuxRuntimeInitChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// Assembly
// ==========================================================================

// Append the x64 fault runtime for `target`'s OS. Called after the module's own functions are encoded, so
// the runtime lays out above user code; `__symtable` closes `.text` after all of it. `macos`/`wasi` never
// reach here — `buildX64Backend` appends this only for windows/linux.
//
// ⭐ THE EXIT ROUTE IS DERIVED HERE, ONCE, FROM THE SAME DOOR EVERY OTHER x64 OS DECISION ASKS
// (`x64OsCallKind`) — it is not re-stated as a literal inside each lane appender. This is the only place
// in the x64 backend that holds a `Target` at the moment the chunk set is chosen, so it is the only place
// that can answer without repeating itself; the lane appenders receive the answer and pass it on.
export function appendX64RuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, target Target, dumpCoverage bool, usesGt bool)
	let exitRoute = x64OsCallKind(target.os)

	match target.os 'os'
		windows then appendX64WindowsRuntimeChunks(chunks, exitRoute: exitRoute, dumpCoverage: dumpCoverage, usesGt: usesGt, target: target)

		// ⚠ Linux needs no `usesGt`: every OS call in its half is a raw `syscall` instruction, which runs on
		// whatever stack it is on and asks the scheduler nothing.
		linux then appendX64LinuxRuntimeChunks(chunks, exitRoute: exitRoute, dumpCoverage: dumpCoverage)
		macos or
			wasi then panic("appendX64RuntimeChunks: no x64 fault runtime for {targetToString(target)} — this is appended only for windows/linux")
	end 'os'
end 'appendX64RuntimeChunks'

// ⭐ THE CHUNKS BOTH LANES NEED, WRITTEN ONCE. A per-lane copy of this list is not a compile error when
// the two drift — a chunk missing from one of them is a diagnostic that simply does not print there,
// under a green suite. Written once, a shared chunk cannot be present on one lane and absent from the
// other, which closes the failure mode rather than one instance of it.
//
// `exitRoute` is the ONE OS fact any of these five contains (`mrt_panic`'s step (4)); every other byte
// they assemble is identical on both lanes, either because the chunk is OS-neutral outright or because it
// reaches the OS BY NAME through `mrt_write_cstr_stderr`, which each lane appends its own writer under.
//
// ⚠ ORDER AMONG RUNTIME CHUNKS IS INERT and this function relies on it: they all lay out inside
// `[mrt_start, __symtable)`, which is the only range `emitTextBoundsGuard` tests, and every reference
// between them is a name resolved by the intra-`.text` call fixup rather than a distance. Verified by
// running both suites and the panic probes across the reordering this function introduced.
function appendX64SharedRuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, exitRoute X64OsCallKind, dumpCoverage bool)
	chunks.push(buildPanicChunk(exitRoute, dumpCoverage: dumpCoverage))
	chunks.push(buildFaultBacktraceChunk())
	chunks.push(buildWalkFramesChunk())
	chunks.push(buildPrintFrameChunk())
	chunks.push(buildWriteHexChunk())
end 'appendX64SharedRuntimeChunks'

// The Windows-ONLY half of the fault runtime: the VEH `runtime_init` + `fault_thunk`, plus the ONE stream
// writer every producer in the program reaches and the C-string front end the diagnostics call it through.
// Everything else it needs comes from the shared appender above.
//
// ⚠ `usesGt` travels this far because `mrt_write_stream` calls the IAT by hand, and a hand-assembled call
// is not routed through the green-thread shim by `emitFunctionChunk` the way a compiled function's is.
function appendX64WindowsRuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, exitRoute X64OsCallKind, dumpCoverage bool, usesGt bool, target Target)
	chunks.push(buildRuntimeInitChunk())
	chunks.push(buildFaultThunkChunk())
	chunks.push(buildWriteStreamChunk(usesGt, target: target))
	chunks.push(buildWriteStderrChunk())
	appendX64SharedRuntimeChunks(chunks, exitRoute: exitRoute, dumpCoverage: dumpCoverage)
end 'appendX64WindowsRuntimeChunks'

// The Linux-ONLY half: the rt_sigaction `runtime_init`, the sigreturn trampoline and the SIGFPE
// `fault_handler`, over a `write`-syscall stderr writer published under the same
// `mrt_write_cstr_stderr` label the Windows writer uses — which is what lets the shared chunks call it
// without knowing which lane they are on.
function appendX64LinuxRuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, exitRoute X64OsCallKind, dumpCoverage bool)
	chunks.push(buildLinuxRuntimeInitChunk())
	chunks.push(buildLinuxSigreturnTrampolineChunk())
	chunks.push(buildLinuxFaultHandlerChunk())
	chunks.push(buildLinuxWriteStderrChunk())
	appendX64SharedRuntimeChunks(chunks, exitRoute: exitRoute, dumpCoverage: dumpCoverage)
end 'appendX64LinuxRuntimeChunks'
